# Workflow Rules

Business workflows must use explicit persisted states.

Do not use scattered booleans to represent workflow state when a state machine is more appropriate.

Initial workflow states include:

NEW
QUALIFYING
QUALIFIED
AVAILABILITY_CHECK
SLOT_OFFERED
BOOKING_PENDING
BOOKED
CONFIRMED
JOB_PENDING
JOB_COMPLETED
FOLLOW_UP
CLOSED

Exception states include:

WAITING_FOR_CUSTOMER
WAITING_FOR_CONTRACTOR
WAITING_FOR_HUMAN
OUTSIDE_SERVICE_AREA
NO_AVAILABILITY
EMERGENCY
UNKNOWN_REQUEST
INTEGRATION_FAILURE
BOOKING_FAILED
CUSTOMER_DECLINED

Only explicitly permitted state transitions may occur.

Never allow arbitrary state transitions from AI-generated text.

Every important transition should produce an auditable workflow event.

Workflow state must be persisted.

Do not rely on in-memory state for long-running processes.

External operations must not automatically imply successful workflow transitions.

For example:

Checking calendar availability does not mean a booking exists.

A booking becomes BOOKED only after the booking operation has been successfully confirmed.

If an external operation fails, preserve the workflow state and record the failure.

Handle duplicate events and webhook deliveries safely.

Use idempotency for mutating operations where appropriate.

Never silently discard workflow failures.

Every workflow should have a clear terminal state or human escalation path.
